 Linux Inter‑Process Communication Using Pipes and FIFO
⏰ Linux Alarm Clock System

A Linux-based Alarm Clock System implemented in C that demonstrates core Operating Systems concepts such as process creation, signals, inter-process communication, concurrency, synchronization, memory management, and file I/O.

The system allows users to create, manage, monitor, cancel, snooze, and schedule multiple alarms while the operating system continues handling other activities concurrently.

📌 Project Overview

The Linux Alarm Clock System is designed to demonstrate how an operating system manages processes, timers, signals, inter-process communication, memory, files, and concurrent activities.

Instead of simply waiting in a loop until an alarm time is reached, the system creates separate alarm processes and uses Linux mechanisms such as fork(), alarm(), SIGALRM, pipes, FIFOs, POSIX threads, mutexes, condition variables, and semaphores.

The project also provides persistent alarm storage, recurring alarms, alarm analytics, missed-alarm detection, daily scheduling, and sound notifications.

