🧠 Operating System Concepts Demonstrated

CO-1: Operating System as a Service Layer

The application interacts with Linux through system calls and OS services.

Examples:

getpid()
getppid()
getuid()
getgid()
sysconf()
open()
write()
close()


CO-2: Processes and Process Control

Each alarm can be handled by a separate child process.

Important system calls and concepts:

fork()
waitpid()
kill()
setpgid()
Parent and child processes
Process IDs
Process groups
Process termination

Process flow:

Parent Process
      |
    fork()
      |
      +--------------------+
      |                    |
 Parent Process       Child Alarm Process
                           |
                        alarm()
                           |
                        pause()
                           |
                       SIGALRM
                           |
                    Alarm Notification


CO-3: Inter-Process Communication

The project demonstrates multiple IPC mechanisms:

Anonymous Pipe

Used for communication between alarm child processes and the monitoring thread.

pipe(event_pipe);
Named FIFO
mkfifo("alarm_control_fifo", 0666);

Used for sending control commands such as:

CANCEL <alarm_id>
Signals

The project uses:

SIGALRM
SIGTERM

SIGALRM is used to notify an alarm process when its scheduled time is reached.


CO-4: Memory Management

Dynamic memory is used to store alarms.

Functions include:

malloc()
realloc()
free()

The alarm list dynamically grows as more alarms are created.

The project also demonstrates the relationship between process creation and virtual memory through fork() and copy-on-write.


CO-5: File Systems and File I/O

The system uses Linux file operations for persistent storage and logging.

Important functions:

open()
write()
close()

Files used:

alarms.dat
alarm_history.txt

alarms.dat stores alarm information, while alarm_history.txt records important alarm events.


CO-6: Concurrency and Synchronization

The project uses POSIX threads and synchronization mechanisms.

Implemented concepts:

POSIX threads
Mutex
Condition variable
Semaphore
Concurrent alarm monitoring
Synchronization of shared alarm data

Important functions:

pthread_create()
pthread_mutex_lock()
pthread_mutex_unlock()
pthread_cond_wait()
pthread_cond_signal()
sem_wait()
sem_post()

A separate monitoring thread continuously checks alarm events while the main program remains available for user interaction.


